{"authored" [{"question" "q-40168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3b9ca1f0e5e88294dd232877ed23203a149809a11e92ceac8c681f06ab8309f" "basis" 2382 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3b9ca1f0e5e88294dd232877ed23203a149809a11e92ceac8c681f06ab8309f" "basis" 2382 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-40168" "field" nil "attribute" "que